1
00:00:00,137 --> 00:00:10,588
This is team Divinecodes, presenting Project Two: mileage forecasting and erroneous mileage detection. We built

2
00:00:10,588 --> 00:00:18,841
a local pipeline and an interactive demonstration to estimate a rental car's current mileage, forecast

3
00:00:18,841 --> 00:00:18,941
future mileage, and identify reports that deserve a closer look

4
00:00:18,938 --> 00:00:26,475
During a long rental, the latest recorded odometer may be weeks out of date. SIXT

5
00:00:26,475 --> 00:00:31,208
still needs to value the car and decide when to remove it from the rental

6
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:42,934
fleet. A nowcast estimates mileage today. A forecast estimates mileage on a future date. Suspicious

7
00:00:42,934 --> 00:00:43,034
reading detection helps analysts find possible entry mistakes or unusual usage

8
00:00:42,223 --> 00:00:50,222
The development sample contains nearly two point seven million status rows across twenty seven thousand

9
00:00:50,222 --> 00:00:59,860
six hundred and thirty vehicles. We added Parquet support and streaming fleet loading. The separate

10
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:08,906
inference file has one hundred and sixty three thousand, forty seven missing mileage targets. We

11
00:01:08,906 --> 00:01:15,630
keep those targets separate from model tuning and do not use the empty organizer prediction

12
00:01:15,630 --> 00:01:15,730
or error columns as features

13
00:01:09,308 --> 00:01:14,350
Our main insight is that the date a mileage value belongs to can differ from

14
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:23,038
the date the system first knows it. Some On Rent rows contain older handover mileage

15
00:01:23,038 --> 00:01:30,789
We retime those values, but never reveal them before their row was recorded. We apply

16
00:01:30,789 --> 00:01:39,531
the same availability boundary to rental metadata and changing vehicle categories. The fleet pattern is

17
00:01:39,531 --> 00:01:39,631
not universal, so we document the assumption rather than treating every row as certain truth

18
00:01:37,657 --> 00:01:45,952
We compared the last recorded reading, an average daily rate, rental aware models, a recent

19
00:01:45,952 --> 00:01:55,603
usage variant, a blend, and a robust rate candidate. Earlier validation chooses the model separately

20
00:01:55,603 --> 00:02:05,930
for each task. A different later period calibrates the prediction ranges. Final evaluation uses later

21
00:02:05,930 --> 00:02:14,597
readings on held out vehicles. Future field mutation tests check that changing future rows cannot

22
00:02:14,597 --> 00:02:14,697
change earlier predictions

23
00:02:07,226 --> 00:02:15,782
Here is the working dashboard. Every vehicle in this demonstration is made up. Select a

24
00:02:15,782 --> 00:02:22,561
car on the left. The first card shows its last recorded mileage and the age

25
00:02:22,561 --> 00:02:31,049
of that reading. The orange card shows the model's current estimate and a prediction range

26
00:02:31,049 --> 00:02:37,663
The timeline connects the known history to the estimate, making the gap visible instead of

27
00:02:37,663 --> 00:02:37,763
hiding the uncertainty

28
00:02:31,751 --> 00:02:40,328
Next, change the forecast horizon from thirty days to ninety days. The future estimate and

29
00:02:40,328 --> 00:02:49,311
its range update using outputs precomputed by the Python models. Longer horizons can require much

30
00:02:49,311 --> 00:02:58,798
wider ranges. The page also supports searching, filtering, and exporting the selected estimate as a

31
00:02:58,798 --> 00:02:58,898
CSV. The demo works offline and needs no API key

32
00:02:56,906 --> 00:03:05,449
The review queue includes an intentionally invented mileage spike. Each signal shows the report date

33
00:03:05,449 --> 00:03:15,604
the reading, and a reason for investigation. Our rules cover isolated spikes or dips, decreases

34
00:03:15,604 --> 00:03:27,681
implausible rates, movement without recorded rentals, unchanged readings during rental days, and model residual outliers

35
00:03:27,681 --> 00:03:34,246
A flag is a request for review. It is not proof of fraud, and real

36
00:03:34,246 --> 00:03:34,346
precision still requires domain expert labels

37
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:35,199
On held out development readings, current mileage error falls from five hundred and sixty one

38
00:03:35,199 --> 00:03:43,556
kilometres for the last reading baseline to four hundred and fifteen. Forecast error falls from

39
00:03:43,556 --> 00:03:51,837
seven thousand, three hundred and seventy six kilometres to two thousand, seven hundred and thirty

40
00:03:51,837 --> 00:04:00,676
four. Those are reductions of twenty six percent and sixty two point nine percent. The

41
00:04:00,676 --> 00:04:10,507
selected models also beat the stronger average rate baseline. These are fleet evaluation results, separate

42
00:04:10,507 --> 00:04:10,607
from the synthetic dashboard

43
00:04:01,473 --> 00:04:08,342
In a controlled development benchmark, the detector caught four hundred and fifty one of four

44
00:04:08,342 --> 00:04:18,400
hundred and seventy nine injected mistakes: ninety four point two percent episode recall. Exact corrupted

45
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:26,667
reading recall was ninety three point nine percent. Episode recall can also credit the reading

46
00:04:26,667 --> 00:04:36,677
immediately before a rollback. This does not establish real world precision. Untouched readings may already

47
00:04:36,677 --> 00:04:36,777
contain errors, and some flags may reflect missing or stale operational data

48
00:04:32,821 --> 00:04:39,945
We filled all one hundred and sixty three thousand, forty seven missing mileage targets and

49
00:04:39,945 --> 00:04:48,811
preserved the original table. Most use the vehicle's own earlier history with a range. Three

50
00:04:48,811 --> 00:04:56,093
thousand, seven hundred and forty five have no usable own history, so they receive an

51
00:04:56,093 --> 00:05:06,207
explicitly flagged fleet cohort fallback. Those fallbacks have no calibrated range and need review. The

52
00:05:06,207 --> 00:05:06,307
